00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.570
OK, yeah, just in the way
of a brief introduction,

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.390
I'll leave a lot of
what's on the slide,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.060
in case you're going
to be delivering

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.690
the slides intact at
a later point in time.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.640
For the most part, just
wanted to summarize,

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.160
I'm currently at the
Ohio State University

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.800
and have been there since 2004.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.100
However, I actually finished
my doctorate work there back

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:11.910
in the '90s.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.880
And so I've been associated
with the Ohio State

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.713
University for quite some time.

00:01:16.713 --> 00:01:18.630
I've taught at the Fisher
College of Business.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.420
I'm now in the College
of engineering.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.280
I've actually run almost all of
the various interdisciplinary

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.730
hybrid experiential educational
programs in innovation

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.080
and entrepreneurship.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.150
Some of them are
now on their own.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.060
And I'm not involved
with them any longer,

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.760
the Langdale TEC Academy
and a variety of others.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.150
I'm currently mostly responsible
for the I-Corps@Ohio program,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.820
which trains research
scientists and graduate students

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.150
in the advanced principles of
technology entrepreneurship.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.820
And I'm working on some
undergraduate programming

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.250
within the Center for Design
and Manufacturing Excellence

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.260
on innovation and technology.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.080
Entrepreneurship,
there's not a big focus.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.210
If you can believe that,
with such a big school,

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.250
there's not a big focus
from the undergraduates

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.580
in the engineering program
on entrepreneurship.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:08.360
So we thought we'd better course
correct there pretty quickly.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.815
So anyway, there's more detail
there on the slide for you.

00:02:12.815 --> 00:02:14.190
You know, it was
interesting when

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:21.420
I got asked to share with you
this evening, I was a bit--

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.120
I guess, the best
word is perplexed.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.950
Because of the notion
of what I understand

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.590
art entrepreneurship
to be and what

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.715
the general theme of
entrepreneurship is.

00:02:30.715 --> 00:02:32.340
And I thought what
might be a great way

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.700
to start so that we make sure
we're all on the same page is

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.890
just to start with
what I found to be

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.752
a pretty interesting
definition of entrepreneurship.

00:02:40.752 --> 00:02:42.210
The field of
entrepreneurship, it's

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.250
been around since
the '70s for the most

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.160
part as an academic discipline.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.250
And to a large extent,
the definitions

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:51.720
that we use in
terms of researching

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.670
it and understanding
what we mean by the word

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.280
entrepreneurship or
entrepreneurial behavior

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.990
really has never taken root.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:01.020
There's a lot of disagreement
among the scholars.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.720
And there's a lot
of effort to make

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.540
the word relate to
whatever makes its best

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.080
usefulness for the academic
programs that you're pursuing.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.218
I found this
particular definition.

00:03:12.218 --> 00:03:14.010
It's very consistent
with mine, though it's

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.780
got one small variation.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.610
But this was from James Hart.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:21.463
And I liked it because he's
from the School of Art at SMU.

00:03:21.463 --> 00:03:23.880
He says that entrepreneurship,
the creation of opportunity

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.240
and value with intent to
profit in a variety of ways

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.920
through the assumption
of risk and effort.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.800
And one of the few examples
that, like me, actually focuses

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.390
on the assumption of risk.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:40.120
And I was asked to speak
on value proposition.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.080
This is a really tough
topic to speak on.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.630
And I'll share as we go through
here in just a few minutes

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.093
why it is very challenging.

00:03:49.093 --> 00:03:50.760
But for the most part,
it's because it's

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.570
at the center of what
entrepreneurship means,

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:57.510
the notion of both creating
and capturing value

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:02.155
and also at the same time
executing the discipline

00:04:02.155 --> 00:04:04.530
and the mechanics of actually
running a business in order

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.310
to capture that value.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:08.550
These are the
critical components

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:12.690
that are very challenging
to actually identify.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.670
They're very
challenging to evaluate.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.899
And they're very
challenging to execute.

00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:19.920
And so for me, this is
the bread and butter

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.200
of all entrepreneurship,
regardless of art

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.570
entrepreneurship or otherwise.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:29.040
And I thought to myself, if
you'll give me the permission,

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.800
I'm going to just
venture off here

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.800
for just a second to talk
about what I think of when I

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.670
think of arts entrepreneurship.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:40.040
If you take the definition
we just identified,

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.800
there's really three
ways I can slice it.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.200
And I look at the
entrepreneurial effort

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:47.090
as the enterprise
in the arts industry

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:49.220
as one component
that is established

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:51.590
in the means by
which we buy and sell

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.140
art or artistic expression.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:57.200
And I would think of
examples like Shutterfly.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.210
I know that the
competition you're in now

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:02.150
is a strong focus on the
role that technology plays

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:06.530
in advancing both the creation
and distribution and trade

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.150
of art itself.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.340
I think this is one
particular context that

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.980
makes an interesting
or would provide

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:14.690
an interesting
perspective in terms

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:15.898
of what we're thinking about.

00:05:15.898 --> 00:05:18.110
I'm sure several of
your teams are actually

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.730
focusing on arts
entrepreneurship

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.910
in this regard, the means of
buying and selling and using

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:25.080
technology to exploit that.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.490
However, there's also the notion
of enterprise for the arts.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:31.580
And I thought it was a nice
example of Michelangelo

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:33.620
with the Sistine Chapel.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.230
You probably know well, he
was actually paid to do that

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:39.410
and in fact, had to be
bribed significantly in order

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:41.090
to leave a job he
already had that he

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:43.430
was being paid for in
order to take on the job

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.920
and was paid what
was, at the time,

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:49.310
pretty handsomely what
took four years to create.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.350
So we'll talk a
little bit in a moment

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:54.050
about the notion
of value creation

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:59.238
and how time, investment, cost,
opportunity all come into play

00:05:59.238 --> 00:06:01.280
and have to be considered
in terms of our venture

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.640
opportunities as to whether the
value we're actually creating,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:06.230
the value proposition,
which we're

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:08.510
developing within
our enterprises

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:12.950
are really worth the
opportunity or worth the effort.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:17.000
And thirdly, the notion
of enterprise as art.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.860
This is using our
creative expression

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:23.570
to create sustainable and
long lasting solutions.

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:28.210
For me, this is the essence of
what I do in entrepreneurship.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.610
I never would have called it
arts entrepreneurship or don't

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:33.410
call it arts entrepreneurship.

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:38.420
But for me, the art is actually
in the design and creation

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:41.510
of the underlying
business model enterprise

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:45.260
and how you can solve unique
and challenging problems

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.590
in the world through
the power of enterprise.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:52.550
And I believe just like
a canvas of an artist,

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:57.020
my business model is a canvas
by which I can create and make

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:00.080
lasting impact and significant
meaning through the work

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:01.290
that I do.

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.990
And so for me, I've
had opportunities

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:06.650
to develop thousands
of artistic expression

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:09.560
says enterprise as art itself.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.380
And I thought this was just
a nice way to characterize,

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:14.878
depending on where the
teams are coming from.

00:07:14.878 --> 00:07:15.920
I wouldn't have any idea.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.960
I haven't read any of your
original idea pitches.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.200
And I probably won't
be there at the end

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:25.598
to see those pitches at the
end of the final program.

00:07:25.598 --> 00:07:27.140
So just wanted to
share that with you

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:31.460
as a way of contextualizing
what we're talking about.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:34.400
For me, I also am
fairly simplistic.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.410
I like to break down the
concepts of something

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:38.093
like value proposition
so once again,

00:07:38.093 --> 00:07:39.760
to make sure we're
all on the same page.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:41.510
So while we have
multiple contexts

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:44.630
in which to think about
how we create value

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.180
through our enterprising
initiatives,

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.490
what is it that we're
really creating when we

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.700
talk about a value proposition?

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:55.520
And simply put,
the notion of value

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.210
is the importance worth
or usefulness of whatever

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.310
the target focus is,
whatever the thing

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:02.900
is we're talking about.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:04.790
The proposition is the
notion of a statement

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:08.960
that expresses that concept
and can be true or false.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:11.780
And when I mentioned
earlier the notion of risk,

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:14.270
therein lies the
essence of risk in terms

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:16.530
of how we create value.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:20.202
In this instance, our value
propositions, the proposals,

00:08:20.202 --> 00:08:21.660
if you will, the
propositions we're

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:24.030
making regarding how our
new enterprises are going

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:27.630
to create value are
actually statements

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:31.080
that express the
concept at its essence

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:35.429
but also can be true or false.

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:37.409
And that's the
critical distinction.

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:39.659
What we want to talk about
this evening is regardless

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:41.280
of context in
terms of how you're

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:43.409
using your creative
expression to drive value

00:08:43.409 --> 00:08:46.160
through your
enterprising initiatives,

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.650
the key here is that
the basis on which you

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:51.140
determine what the
value proposition is

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.120
is either true or false.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.830
And essentially, that's
what it comes down to.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:58.900
The fundamentals of
value proposition--

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.100
though, I wish we had time.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:03.970
I literally teach an entire
month of a 2 and 1/2 month

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:07.840
course on just this concept.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:10.970
Typically, we're working with
high technology businesses.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:15.460
However, much of that technology
embeds the creative expression

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:17.470
in the research
scientists who have

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:19.930
developed those inventions.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:22.510
And so there's a strong
creative expression

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:26.050
that's actually undergirding all
of the initiatives that we do.

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.370
And in understanding the
value of the proposition,

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:30.610
you have to ask yourself
these key considerations

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:32.860
or the key
fundamental questions.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:34.960
In other words, the
risk is, if you go back

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.860
to the definition earlier,
the notion of the effort,

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.840
the risk associated.

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:43.810
This question is is it feasible?

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:46.150
It's one thing to have
invented something in a lab

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:48.130
or to be tinkering
with something at home

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:51.580
and identify a unique way
to manipulate technology

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:54.020
to create different outcomes.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:56.020
The question, however,
in value proposition

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:58.780
is can we really create
sustainable value?

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:01.270
What is the value
creative potential

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:03.880
of what we've developed
or what we've discovered?

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:05.470
Is it feasible?

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:09.160
And that's determined by based
on the notion of time and cost

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.530
associated with its achievement.

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:15.520
Again, we can't simply stop with
that first initial discovery.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:18.033
We have to understand that if
value is going to be created,

00:10:18.033 --> 00:10:19.450
it's going to be
solving problems.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.280
And if so, it's going
to be introduced

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.910
into the marketplace.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:25.930
And so the notion of the risk
or the time and cost associated

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:29.170
with making it feasible has
to be taken into consideration

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:32.230
when we're thinking
of value propositions.

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:35.050
It's also the notion
of value, itself.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:36.370
Go figure.

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:39.370
But in this case, the
question is is it different?

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:43.870
In other words, does it create
new value, net new value?

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:45.970
And that value was
created by the degree

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:48.520
to which the solution
addresses the core parameters

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:49.600
of the problem.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:52.270
And you may say
resolves the problem

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:55.810
or resolves the core components
of the problem, perhaps not

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:57.190
all the components.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:01.600
You can imagine a number of
solutions, artistic or not,

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.370
that solve some portion
of a particular problem

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.210
but only address that
particular piece and that needs

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:11.710
other solutions in order to
derive the total resolution.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:15.100
And the third and final
is is it necessary?

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:17.370
In other words, is
it necessary means

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:21.900
is it likely to be adopted
or utilized and deployed?

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:25.560
And will there be an
opportunity for this notion

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:28.110
of what we call the trade, the
transaction activity, where

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:31.380
we capture the
value that we embed

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:35.340
or create within our products
and services solutions?

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:38.460
And that is represented in
the buy and sell transaction

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:42.120
activity that happens around
those new products and services

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:43.590
that we introduced.

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:45.720
So think of your idea.

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:47.430
Think of the
creative idea you're

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:51.150
considering now as part
of this competition.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:53.520
Is it feasible?

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:56.120
How is it different,
if it's different?

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:58.640
And why is it necessary?

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.770
And you'll find that these
are critical questions

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:05.210
that if you can answer, not
only increase your confidence

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:08.600
in the enterprising initiative
you're trying to undergird

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:11.720
with the art
expression, you'll also

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:14.330
find that it's the
essential element

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:17.270
to build your confidence
to move forward.

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:19.070
And those are the
key considerations in

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:22.280
whether or not you ever do
start this venture you're

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:23.480
playing with at this time.

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:29.290
This is an interesting
quote in the process

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:31.480
of trying to find
this illustration

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:33.910
to drive home the point
about the different context.

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.790
I came across the quote
from Michelangelo.

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:38.860
And I thought it was intriguing.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:40.600
"If you knew how much
work went into it,

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:42.310
you would not call it genius."

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:46.220
Now, if any of you are
art majors or art students

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:48.670
or professionals, you probably
have come across this.

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:50.630
It might not mean much to you.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:55.870
But in the context of value
proposition and value creation,

00:12:55.870 --> 00:13:00.550
this is a very interesting
quote to decipher.

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:02.410
Again, we won't spend
much time with it.

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:04.840
But think about what
that means with respect

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:08.440
to the notion of
the overall art that

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:13.300
was created, the effort that
had to go in to create this art.

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:15.880
And yes, by the way, as
I mentioned, he was paid

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:20.730
and was paid well
according to the times.

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:24.130
But what about the trade,
the buy-sell concept?

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:25.040
Was it worth it?

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:27.490
Do you believe there
was value captured?

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:29.590
Now we look at this
piece of art today,

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:31.730
and we see the intrinsic value.

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:34.150
I mean, it moves
your soul or should

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:37.820
or perhaps, or that
was the intent.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:44.080
But does it ultimately
have extrinsic value?

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:46.950
And would it have been
perceived as such at the time?

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.550
When I start to translate
some of these concepts

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:55.650
into the type of
ventures that I start

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:57.600
or help to start
on a regular basis,

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:59.940
whether it be through my
students or the research

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:02.160
scientist that we're
training or some

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:06.150
of the consulting activities,
just like artists use a canvas,

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:09.180
we use what we call the
Business Model Canvas.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:12.420
And this is a very
simple rendering

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:14.740
of what the Business
Model Canvas is.

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:18.540
And like most of these
types of illustrations,

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:21.900
they really are in
and of themselves

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:26.500
of any particular
insight or value.

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.020
The importance of this
Canvas is it's simply

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:32.470
a way of organizing
your information.

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:36.220
Ultimately, in order to
articulate a clear, compelling

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:38.740
value proposition,
you have to be

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:42.820
able to show how it embeds
within an overarching business

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:44.210
model.

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:47.870
It's not enough to just simply
describe the value proposition.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:51.820
In fact, it's not
possible in and of itself.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:53.920
If you see how the
value proposition is

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:57.870
positioned within this canvas,
it's directly in the center.

00:14:57.870 --> 00:15:01.950
And immediately to the right
is the customer segments

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:05.580
and all the ways in which
the value proposition serves

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:08.310
the needs of those
customer segments, again,

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:10.470
depending on the
nature of that segment

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:13.290
and the nature of the
product service offering.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:15.770
There's a variety of ways in
which value proposition serves

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:16.270
it.

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.820
But if you look
immediately to the left,

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:22.800
these are all the ways in which
we create the value proposition

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:27.570
that solves and serves the
needs of the customer segments.

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:30.630
Those value creating
competencies

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:34.380
around key activities, key
resources, and key partnerships

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:37.900
are the critical backbone of
the notion of value proposition.

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:40.800
So it's not enough to have a
compelling value proposition.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.750
It's only compelling
to the extent

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:44.832
we believe you can deliver.

00:15:44.832 --> 00:15:46.290
And the only way
we can believe you

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:48.055
can deliver if you
clearly understand

00:15:48.055 --> 00:15:49.680
what the key activities
are that you're

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:52.110
going to need to master in
order to deliver that value

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:55.110
proposition and what
key resources you're

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:58.140
going to have to have in
order to sustain that,

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:00.630
and where you don't have
those resources, what

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:02.520
key partnerships are
you going to have

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.290
to build into your
overarching model

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:07.080
in order to deliver
that path ultimately

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:10.620
to drive solutions to your
customers that are better

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:15.310
than alternative competitive
solutions on the market now.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:17.560
So this is really an
organizing framework.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:19.680
But for me, from an
artistic standpoint,

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:24.630
I envision it as a very
powerful and compelling canvas

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:26.460
by which we can--

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:31.260
or lump of clay by which we can
sculpt a compelling business

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:32.040
model.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:34.240
And it might sound
like a play on words,

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:35.880
but I actually do
consider myself

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:38.250
to be fairly arts-oriented.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:40.560
I come from a
family of that sort.

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:42.760
And I'm actually,
personality-wise,

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:45.000
much of a designer,
builder type.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:48.360
And I'm constantly, whether I'm
building educational programs

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:52.350
or building startup
opportunities or new ventures,

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:57.060
there's always a design
and creative articulation

00:16:57.060 --> 00:17:00.757
that is embedded in
all of that work.

00:17:00.757 --> 00:17:03.090
What we're driving for here
in a clear, compelling sense

00:17:03.090 --> 00:17:04.859
is this notion of fit.

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:09.750
We want to understand clearly
how our solution fits the needs

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:12.750
or solves the problems
in a compelling way

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:14.332
with the customer segments.

00:17:14.332 --> 00:17:16.290
And I'm going to come
back to this at least one

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.780
other time, if not
more, and we'll

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:22.020
explain there's actually
more to that story.

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:23.940
But we have to develop
our thoughts here

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:25.869
for just a moment.

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:30.600
When you get this deck,
this will be a slide

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:32.220
that you can use as
a reference slide.

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.530
I'm not going to
pour over this slide.

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:36.240
This was actually
from an article

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:38.340
I used in the MBA class.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:41.730
I thought it was nicely
summarized as a way

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:44.880
to consider what a
value proposition is,

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:48.600
what it consists of, and
what challenges it addresses

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:51.420
for the customer, what
is required in order

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.502
for it to be compelling, and
what are some of the challenges

00:17:54.502 --> 00:17:55.710
and risks associated with it.

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:57.870
And then you see
how that plays out

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.070
across the different columns.

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:01.540
What I'd like you to--

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:03.270
what I would like to
point out and what

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:05.790
I hope you'll discern when
you spend a little bit of time

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:11.370
with this is the notion of
value propositions, direct link

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:15.150
and time consideration of
the customer and the customer

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:16.660
segment.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:21.250
And it's not enough to simply
know who your customer is.

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:26.410
And while this is a nice
additional-- a nice summary

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:29.710
resource to use, it falls
short in what I believe

00:18:29.710 --> 00:18:31.960
to be a critical component.

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:35.350
But once you know that component
and you read this chart

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:40.810
through that lens, you'll
have a deep and comprehensive

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:43.960
perspective of what these terms
mean and how relevant they are.

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:47.620
And we're going to talk about
that perspective in a moment.

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:50.170
Before we jump to that,
are there any questions

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:54.130
at all about what we
mean by value proposition

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:56.950
and what we mean by the
context of value proposition

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:59.592
creation and arts and
arts entrepreneurship?

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:04.350
If people have questions,
they can either

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:08.790
unmute to ask Dr.
Camp, or I will happily

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:10.140
receive those through the chat.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:15.670
OK.

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:18.420
Looks like everything is
smooth sailing so far.

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:20.400
So I will just let you continue.

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:21.360
Very good.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:26.040
I think it would be fun to just
talk about a good illustration.

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:28.890
I'm sorry, I don't have a
better one at the moment

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:31.680
to match up with
the arts competition

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:32.910
that you're going to.

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:34.560
As I've said
already though, I do

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:37.770
believe that there's
enormous creative expression,

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:42.390
enormous use of technology
in a lot of product markets

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:43.840
that I spend time.

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:47.550
My work for has crossed over 36
different technology verticals

00:19:47.550 --> 00:19:52.020
from the medical to the
software to materials

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:53.940
and ag-related bioproducts.

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:59.070
And so I don't have a problem
transitioning or perhaps

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:01.410
the better word is
translating these concepts

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:04.650
from one segment to the next.

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:08.790
And yet I see this as an
enormous expression of--

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:10.320
or an illustration
that expresses

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:13.830
the enormous potential in
the world of value creation.

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:16.913
If you'll look at the
notion of the Keurig Kold--

00:20:16.913 --> 00:20:19.080
I don't know if anybody's
familiar with Keurig Kold.

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:21.630
And I wish I could see you,
so you could raise your hand.

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:23.890
But it's not that important.

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:26.490
The critical consideration
is you're probably all

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:28.830
familiar with the Keurig Hot.

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:31.770
Well, before there was a Keurig
Hot, there was just Keurig.

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:34.110
And Keurig Hot came to
be because of the Keurig

00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:37.440
Kold and the notion of
having to differentiate

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:40.770
how you get a hot beverage
versus you get a cold beverage.

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:44.310
And this was a partnership
that was launched with Keurig

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:49.410
in partnership with Coca-Cola to
develop the same type of K-Cup

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:51.870
type technology
that would deliver

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:56.880
a cold carbonated beverage
as opposed to a hot beverage.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:21:01.470
And I just wondered if anybody
was familiar with that.

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:04.860
Very, very interesting, very,
very exciting technology.

00:21:04.860 --> 00:21:06.510
Actually, a beautiful design.

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:08.940
Again, in my opinion,
extremely artistic,

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:10.260
extremely well-laid-out.

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:13.770
There over 150
issued patents inside

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:18.030
of the Keurig Kold that was
developed along the way.

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:20.130
However, so the
bottom line, there's

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:21.750
really fundamentally
nothing wrong

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:24.900
with this concept in
its technical sense.

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:27.660
Fundamentally, it can
perform if we knew

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:29.850
that it was feasible to work.

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:32.310
This is actually an
illustration taken actually

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:34.710
directly from one of those
patents, the patent around

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:40.320
how to put the carbon dioxide
directly into the solution

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:43.260
in order to make the
carbonated beverage.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:44.940
All of those little
individual pieces

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:47.040
are actually identified
in the patent.

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:50.640
So a very interesting
piece of technology

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:53.640
but one of the most disastrous
new product innovations

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:54.970
of all time.

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:57.120
I mean, ranks up
there as a classic.

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.490
Even if you haven't
heard of Keurig Kold,

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:01.620
if you understand the
facts behind this case,

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:06.210
you know exactly how
disastrous this was.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:09.750
It was pulled off the
market in less than 9 months

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:12.150
about just shy of six years.

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:15.060
And $1 billion went into
the research and development

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:16.860
of the product.

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:18.930
It was taken off the
market less than one

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:21.010
year after the company
had already been acquired.

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:23.340
So even in the acquisition
of the company, the process

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:26.140
with due diligence, this product
would have been evaluated.

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:28.770
And so even the
acquirers would have not

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:31.710
known that this was going to be
as disastrous at the time they

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:32.880
acquired it.

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:35.550
But more importantly,
135 employees

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:38.550
in the state of
Vermont lost their job.

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:41.610
And that's pretty unfortunate.

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:45.490
And I would ask,
so what went wrong?

00:22:45.490 --> 00:22:49.240
But we could probably identify,
just brainstorming, a half

00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:51.710
a dozen different concepts.

00:22:51.710 --> 00:22:55.630
I could tell you that the cost
of each individual 8 ounce

00:22:55.630 --> 00:23:00.100
glass of carbonated
beverage was about $1.32.

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:02.830
The machine was about twice
as big as a Keurig Hot,

00:23:02.830 --> 00:23:07.540
so it literally takes up a
third of your kitchen counter.

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:09.460
It's also extremely
heavy, and there

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:13.000
are very few K-Cups that were
actually available at the time.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:14.650
There were several
Coca-Cola products,

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:16.420
but that was about
it and a few other.

00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:18.380
But that was about it.

00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:20.260
But the bottom line
is there really

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:23.120
was no problem-solution fit.

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:26.160
In other words, if you would've
asked any of these engineers,

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:30.130
you can bet they had a very
strong value proposition.

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:31.360
They were convinced.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:35.770
Even Keurig and Coca-Cola, two
of the top consumer beverage

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:39.730
brands in the world who have
millions of customers and could

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:42.833
go on to any number of those
customers to get a clear,

00:23:42.833 --> 00:23:45.250
compelling sense of whether
or not this product solved any

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:48.110
problem or not, and they didn't.

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:50.560
And if they did, they were
biased in their approach.

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:53.260
Because it didn't show any
evidence that no one would

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:54.680
want to buy.

00:23:54.680 --> 00:23:57.777
Oh, and by the way, the
machine was about $650

00:23:57.777 --> 00:23:59.360
when it first came
out, so about twice

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:02.000
as expensive as the Keurig
Hot when it first came out

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:06.140
and even more so by the
time it was widely adopted.

00:24:06.140 --> 00:24:08.360
So let's not get
into any confusion

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:12.020
that any one of these elements
is what was the downfall.

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:14.150
The reality is
they were building

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:16.350
a product nobody wanted.

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:19.510
And this is a critical
consideration.

00:24:19.510 --> 00:24:23.430
And I know you're only at
the idea stage of your arts

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:25.620
entrepreneurship competition.

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:28.530
But I will tell you that
it's at that stage where

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:30.870
all startups begin.

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:36.120
And the need for or
intention to go beyond that

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:40.710
must be driven by your clear
understanding of the problem

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:42.540
and solution fit.

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:45.480
You remember, the value
proposition, again,

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:48.210
is this importance or worth,
their usefulness of something.

00:24:48.210 --> 00:24:49.410
Let's take the Keurig Kold.

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:55.280
Its importance was dependent
on some key considerations.

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:58.340
And those statements
of importance

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:00.630
were either true or false.

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:04.860
And the best time to
determine the validity

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:07.440
of those statements
is now, today.

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:12.060
During the idea stage is a time
in which you not only create

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:15.090
the value proposition, you
validate and confirm the value

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:17.520
proposition along the way.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:20.380
We have this precept
in all of our teaching

00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:21.960
that's a nice quote.

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:23.910
I use quotes for
all of my precepts.

00:25:23.910 --> 00:25:26.152
But and it's from
Mark Twain that it

00:25:26.152 --> 00:25:28.360
ain't what you don't know
that gets you into trouble.

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:31.230
It's what you know for
sure that just ain't so.

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:33.630
So the reality of testing
the value proposition

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:35.970
of your business is not
that this value proposition

00:25:35.970 --> 00:25:39.930
lives in some obscure
reality that somehow we've

00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:42.150
been enlightened in terms
of the needs of our market

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:44.760
and we know exactly
what the customer wants.

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:47.520
The reality is that's all
driven by the assumptions we're

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:49.830
making, which are
driven by our own biases

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:53.270
and our own ignorance and
the own lack of understanding

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:55.560
of what really happens.

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:57.300
I can point you
to dozens of teams

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:58.967
that were convinced
that they were going

00:25:58.967 --> 00:26:00.720
to solve a particular
problem only

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:05.040
to get testing into the market
and find out the problem didn't

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:07.950
really exist, or the solution
was way over complicated

00:26:07.950 --> 00:26:10.870
for the notion of the problem.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:14.490
So it's important that
we understand and think

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:16.360
about those carefully.

00:26:16.360 --> 00:26:18.900
Let's look at this situation
that has a near zero

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:20.670
probability of being correct.

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:23.430
This is a new venture idea
that was based on the following

00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:24.090
assumptions.

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:26.940
You would probably
recognize it right away.

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:28.680
First assumption is
that there are too

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:33.780
few affordable hotels in the
city for short term stays.

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:36.870
People are probably willing
to pay for alternative options

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:39.122
if the hotels are not available.

00:26:39.122 --> 00:26:40.830
People are probably
willing to compromise

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:42.990
on some of those original
amenities in order just

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:45.130
to save money.

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:47.200
There's probably
enough people that

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:51.130
would be willing to do this
that I could make a profit.

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:53.050
Other people might
even be willing to rent

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:55.150
one or more bedrooms
in their apartments.

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:56.770
And there are
probably enough people

00:26:56.770 --> 00:26:58.450
willing to sub-rent
their apartments

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:00.880
that we might be able to
establish a sustainable

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:03.040
service.

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:08.170
Well, it's not hard
to guess Airbnb.

00:27:08.170 --> 00:27:11.804
And you know that that's
a pretty wild success.

00:27:11.804 --> 00:27:17.500
However, if you understand how
Airbnb actually was created,

00:27:17.500 --> 00:27:20.830
you would realize that each
one of these individual steps

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:23.440
were tested,
actually live tested

00:27:23.440 --> 00:27:26.015
with customers in the
market before they ever

00:27:26.015 --> 00:27:27.640
wrote a business
plan, before they ever

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:29.980
took out a bank loan,
before they ever

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:32.260
decided to find partners.

00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:34.960
They literally just
talked to their friends

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:37.330
and made their rooms
available in their extra rooms

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:40.420
in their apartments, put a
small website together, and then

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:41.500
added more rooms.

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:44.320
And by the time
they ever wrote what

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:46.600
might be considered
a business plan,

00:27:46.600 --> 00:27:48.770
they were already
multi-million dollar business

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:53.140
and already had actually
achieved other successes

00:27:53.140 --> 00:27:54.740
as well.

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:58.300
So but don't fret
about the Keurig Kold.

00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:02.470
Rest assured, Keurig has
figured out all of its problems

00:28:02.470 --> 00:28:04.270
and what went wrong.

00:28:04.270 --> 00:28:09.170
And they've now introduced
Drinkworks Home Bar.

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:12.680
So although the Keurig Kold
was a colossal failure,

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:16.730
Keurig is 100% convinced
that Drinkworks Home Bar,

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:19.760
a single-serve, pod-based
premium cocktail spritzer

00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:23.490
machine, is exactly
what we all want.

00:28:23.490 --> 00:28:25.730
And this is going to go
rolling off the shelves

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:27.780
just any time soon.

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:30.020
These are, yes,
K-Cups that create

00:28:30.020 --> 00:28:32.720
carbonated alcoholic
mixed beverages.

00:28:32.720 --> 00:28:36.770
And that's it, they
figured it out.

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:42.320
Yeah, sure, we'll see.

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:46.550
However, let's bring all of this
back a little closer to home

00:28:46.550 --> 00:28:51.210
and looking at what
we're dealing with.

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:53.580
Another near zero
probability of being

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:56.730
correct set of assumptions
are exactly where

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:59.160
you are right now.

00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:01.560
And in this instance,
with respect

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:05.700
to how your new venture will
create value or capture value,

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:07.590
you're making some
core assumptions.

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:09.870
At least all the teams
that I've coached up

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:13.260
through this point of
25 years of doing this

00:29:13.260 --> 00:29:15.090
typically make these
assumptions going in,

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:18.180
it's why they decided
to enter the competition

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:19.560
in the first place.

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:22.410
There's a level of
confidence in the assumptions

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:26.130
you're making about
your new venture idea.

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:28.680
You are assuming that the
customer you're focused on

00:29:28.680 --> 00:29:30.342
has the problem
you're focused on

00:29:30.342 --> 00:29:31.800
and knows that they
have a problem.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:35.100
Let's hope that they've
tried alternative solutions,

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:38.040
and nothing's worked
up to this point.

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:40.750
They can't articulate
what they need.

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:43.040
That's a really big assumption.

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:46.390
And they know how valuable
an alternative solution would

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:48.590
be if it solved that problem.

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:50.080
And you're probably
already making

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:51.850
assumptions regarding
how much they

00:29:51.850 --> 00:29:55.290
might be willing to pay
for such a solution.

00:29:55.290 --> 00:29:58.070
At the same time, which is
the second side, the risk

00:29:58.070 --> 00:29:59.630
side associated--

00:29:59.630 --> 00:30:02.360
or actually on the
buy-sell side associated

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:04.760
with value proposition,
we're assuming

00:30:04.760 --> 00:30:06.800
that the solution we
can make addresses

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:09.230
those pain points and
performs better than anything

00:30:09.230 --> 00:30:10.940
already in the market.

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:12.980
And it doesn't require
any significant change

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:15.170
in the current workflow
and doesn't increase

00:30:15.170 --> 00:30:17.750
the cost of adopting our
product over alternatives

00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:21.500
and we can sell it for x in
order to sustain the operations

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:24.770
and make a profit
for our new venture.

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:27.560
Some of you could give
your final pitch right now.

00:30:27.560 --> 00:30:30.410
Some of you, based
on these assumptions,

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:35.330
could today write a business
plan and pitch for funding.

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:38.770
It happens all the time.

00:30:38.770 --> 00:30:44.080
But remember that the key to
a strong value proposition--

00:30:44.080 --> 00:30:48.850
the key is the evidence
to determine if it's true.

00:30:48.850 --> 00:30:52.210
The evidence that it's true
is what establishes your value

00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:52.930
proposition.

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:54.670
And remember that
the value proposition

00:30:54.670 --> 00:30:59.980
is the essential ingredient
to your entire business model.

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:01.960
Without a compelling
value proposition,

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:05.020
that you can prove
is true, you really

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:08.320
have left in question the
entire business concept.

00:31:08.320 --> 00:31:11.710
Even though you may have a
lot of other aspects completed

00:31:11.710 --> 00:31:14.680
until this is fully
articulated, there's really not

00:31:14.680 --> 00:31:16.750
much else to be believed.

00:31:16.750 --> 00:31:19.030
There's very little confidence.

00:31:19.030 --> 00:31:23.590
We have a saying in the I-Corps
training program in Ohio.

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:27.350
As I said, as a graduate
student and research scientist

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:30.610
led entrepreneurship
initiative training initiative

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:32.440
that we don't care
what you think.

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:35.530
We're not really even
interested in what you believe.

00:31:35.530 --> 00:31:38.470
The only thing that matters
is what you can prove.

00:31:38.470 --> 00:31:43.250
And that's where value
proposition comes in.

00:31:43.250 --> 00:31:47.470
So we're moving into the last
segment of our few minutes

00:31:47.470 --> 00:31:48.320
together.

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:51.400
Are there any questions about
any of those illustrations

00:31:51.400 --> 00:31:55.000
and the importance of
understanding our customer

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:58.540
segments and why that level
of understanding and depth

00:31:58.540 --> 00:32:01.630
of clarity is necessary
in order to generate

00:32:01.630 --> 00:32:04.260
a good compelling
value proposition?

00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:10.200
So far everything seems
pretty clear to me.

00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:14.670
And I am just desperate to
see how that cocktail Keurig

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:15.510
thing works out.

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:19.890
[LAUGHS] Well, I stopped
short of telling you

00:32:19.890 --> 00:32:22.900
that I had bought one
of those Keutig Kolds.

00:32:22.900 --> 00:32:24.870
However, I bought it
after I knew that it was

00:32:24.870 --> 00:32:26.490
being pulled off the market.

00:32:26.490 --> 00:32:29.650
Because I wanted to use
it as a good example.

00:32:29.650 --> 00:32:31.860
If we were in
class this evening,

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:33.450
I would have it
sitting behind me,

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.390
and you'd get a chance
to at least try that.

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:38.190
But I intend to get
one of these too,

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:40.170
particularly, if it
goes off the market.

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:42.510
But either way, I intend
to get one of these too.

00:32:42.510 --> 00:32:46.350
Excellent, I will look
forward to a full report.

00:32:46.350 --> 00:32:48.460
Very good, very good.

00:32:48.460 --> 00:32:51.990
Well, we're moving now into
a bit granular approach

00:32:51.990 --> 00:32:54.030
to actually
understanding how do we

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:56.980
evaluate the customer and the
customer profile in what we're

00:32:56.980 --> 00:32:57.480
doing.

00:32:57.480 --> 00:33:01.610
And again, I have an
entire 12 week semester

00:33:01.610 --> 00:33:04.380
with finals and
presentations and everything

00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:06.480
around just this concept alone.

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:08.800
And this is at the center
of the I-Corps program.

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:10.500
So we're going to
get a few minutes.

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:12.360
And I apologize we
can't go deeper.

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:14.940
I'm happy to answer any
questions in the future

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:16.230
or our future iterations.

00:33:16.230 --> 00:33:19.110
Or if you want to email me,
I'll make my email available.

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:21.095
So happy to be
helpful, but we're

00:33:21.095 --> 00:33:22.720
going to really cover
it superficially.

00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:27.900
So please ask questions if I
either go too quickly or too

00:33:27.900 --> 00:33:29.730
superficially.

00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:32.070
The notion of the
Business Model Canvas,

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:33.690
as we showed a little bit ago.

00:33:33.690 --> 00:33:36.060
And I mentioned to
you that the key here

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:40.050
is clarifying this value
proposition and its link or fit

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:42.450
with the customer profile
and the pains and problems

00:33:42.450 --> 00:33:44.550
the customer is having in
achieving the jobs they

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:46.680
are intended to achieve.

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:48.390
Well, there's the
same group that

00:33:48.390 --> 00:33:51.870
developed that canvas map,
that Business Model Canvas,

00:33:51.870 --> 00:33:53.760
developed this Value
Proposition Canvas.

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:56.590
And if you have
familiarity with it,

00:33:56.590 --> 00:33:58.800
I'm not going to go into
a deep dive in terms

00:33:58.800 --> 00:34:00.750
of the dynamics of
how the mapping works.

00:34:00.750 --> 00:34:03.480
But I'm going to talk
a little bit about each

00:34:03.480 --> 00:34:05.470
of the individual pieces.

00:34:05.470 --> 00:34:07.890
I didn't want to
bore someone who

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:10.080
maybe uses this in
a number of classes

00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:13.139
or you're using it
now in the program.

00:34:13.139 --> 00:34:17.159
Or I didn't want to
be too loose with any

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:18.330
of these individual pieces.

00:34:18.330 --> 00:34:20.520
Because you maybe
already covered it.

00:34:20.520 --> 00:34:23.310
But all this is is
just a break-out

00:34:23.310 --> 00:34:24.909
of those two segments.

00:34:24.909 --> 00:34:29.190
But we break them out, because
it takes my teams, anyway,

00:34:29.190 --> 00:34:31.630
four out of eight weeks that
when we run the eight week

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:35.130
I-Corp program, it's at least
four weeks before the teams can

00:34:35.130 --> 00:34:37.860
articulate to my satisfaction.

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:40.800
And I'll explain what I mean by
that before they can articulate

00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:43.440
clearly how the value
proposition they create

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:47.429
through the products or services
they offer drive directly value

00:34:47.429 --> 00:34:50.010
created into the
customer for the jobs

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:51.929
he or she's trying to perform.

00:34:51.929 --> 00:34:55.739
And I literally get them down
to that level of clarity.

00:34:55.739 --> 00:34:57.270
And when I say
customers here, I'm

00:34:57.270 --> 00:34:59.490
not talking about,
in this case, just

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:02.820
like the automotive market
or the arts industry

00:35:02.820 --> 00:35:04.560
or the theater program.

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:08.670
I'm literally talking about
who, what job by job title

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:11.790
would be the user,
payer, influencer

00:35:11.790 --> 00:35:14.480
of the purchase of my product
or service as I'm addressing.

00:35:14.480 --> 00:35:18.462
If you can't articulate that,
you leave a big question

00:35:18.462 --> 00:35:19.920
in my mind as to
whether you really

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:22.320
know whether you
create any value or not

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:25.150
or solve any legitimate
problem or not.

00:35:25.150 --> 00:35:27.310
So these are not
abstract concepts.

00:35:27.310 --> 00:35:29.340
These are literally down
to hand-to-hand combat.

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:32.220
We're literally talking face
to face, in this case, Zoom

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:34.240
to Zoom, if you will.

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:37.000
We're talking directly
with our customers.

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:39.420
Let's talk for a moment about
the notion of the customer

00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:39.930
profile.

00:35:39.930 --> 00:35:42.510
This is where you want to
start your customer discovery

00:35:42.510 --> 00:35:45.360
activity as trying to identify
do I have a compelling value

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:47.730
proposition or not.

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:51.300
Is it feasible, is it
different, and is it valuable?

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:53.550
Does it matter?

00:35:53.550 --> 00:35:54.600
Why should it exist?

00:35:54.600 --> 00:35:56.080
These are critical
considerations.

00:35:56.080 --> 00:35:57.663
The first thing you
have to understand

00:35:57.663 --> 00:36:00.390
is you have to explain
who do I assume

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:01.950
is going to use my product.

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:04.590
And then who do I assume,
if my user doesn't

00:36:04.590 --> 00:36:06.870
buy, who's going to buy my
products so my customer can

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:07.590
use it?

00:36:07.590 --> 00:36:09.960
And then if my buyer
doesn't actually use it

00:36:09.960 --> 00:36:12.270
or can't influence
the decision, who

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:15.200
influences the decision
so that my buyer can buy

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:16.920
or so that my
customer can use it.

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:22.110
And sometimes our concepts
are business to consumer.

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:25.110
They're websites, and these
are not complicated matters.

00:36:25.110 --> 00:36:26.790
Other times they're
medical devices.

00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:29.040
And there's 14 levels
of decision maker

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:31.500
you have to go through in
order to get them approved.

00:36:31.500 --> 00:36:34.840
So I'm not sure where
anybody is at any time.

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:37.980
But I can tell you for sure,
if you can't articulate it

00:36:37.980 --> 00:36:43.230
back to me in a simple way and
an effective and compelling

00:36:43.230 --> 00:36:45.900
way, I'm not going to
believe you know it either.

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:49.338
And that's the key what
we're talking about today.

00:36:49.338 --> 00:36:50.880
So in this sense is
these are the set

00:36:50.880 --> 00:36:54.210
of customer characteristics
that in the most important word

00:36:54.210 --> 00:36:57.377
is there that you can
validate with actual customers

00:36:57.377 --> 00:36:57.960
in the market.

00:36:57.960 --> 00:36:59.543
The first thing you
need to understand

00:36:59.543 --> 00:37:00.870
are the customer jobs.

00:37:00.870 --> 00:37:03.660
And that literally is
just asking the customer

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:05.310
that I think has
the problem that

00:37:05.310 --> 00:37:07.880
would use my product, what
is his or her job, what

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:09.170
is it they do?

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:11.770
They're a design engineer at
an automotive manufacturing

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:12.270
facility.

00:37:12.270 --> 00:37:13.430
OK, what do they do?

00:37:13.430 --> 00:37:17.300
OK, they design parts,
They design systems.

00:37:17.300 --> 00:37:18.950
They write software.

00:37:18.950 --> 00:37:20.630
They meet schedules.

00:37:20.630 --> 00:37:22.550
But then I ask the
pains and gains.

00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:24.920
What I want to know there
is, well, what challenges,

00:37:24.920 --> 00:37:28.100
what are the difficulties they
have at getting that job done?

00:37:28.100 --> 00:37:29.668
All their software
is too archaic.

00:37:29.668 --> 00:37:31.460
They have too many
different softwares that

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:33.320
don't link with each other.

00:37:33.320 --> 00:37:34.310
It takes too long.

00:37:34.310 --> 00:37:35.750
It's too much done by committee.

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:38.090
There's no other
design graphic software

00:37:38.090 --> 00:37:39.620
that would make
it more effective,

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:41.060
whatever it might be.

00:37:41.060 --> 00:37:42.842
And then the other is the gains.

00:37:42.842 --> 00:37:44.300
What are the things
they would like

00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:45.950
to achieve but
can't because they

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:49.850
lack the adequate opportunity or
resource, the adequate product

00:37:49.850 --> 00:37:51.980
or solution that
you might provide.

00:37:51.980 --> 00:37:55.190
Maybe they would like to be
able to save time in the designs

00:37:55.190 --> 00:37:59.120
or share the designs with more
of the different designers

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:00.860
across their company
in order to get

00:38:00.860 --> 00:38:02.960
things facilitated more
efficiently or more

00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:04.140
effectively.

00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:05.780
So a host of those things.

00:38:05.780 --> 00:38:08.420
Maybe not the best
example, but you

00:38:08.420 --> 00:38:11.835
need to understand your customer
at a detailed level in terms

00:38:11.835 --> 00:38:14.210
of what job are they trying
to create and what challenges

00:38:14.210 --> 00:38:17.750
and gains are they trying
to achieve with that job.

00:38:17.750 --> 00:38:19.820
And on the value
proposition side,

00:38:19.820 --> 00:38:24.020
the notion here is that do we
have a set of characteristics

00:38:24.020 --> 00:38:27.830
that actually allow the
customer to achieve those gains

00:38:27.830 --> 00:38:32.450
or resolve those pains
associated with their jobs.

00:38:32.450 --> 00:38:34.190
And remember
ultimately, though, we

00:38:34.190 --> 00:38:35.990
have to be able
to do this better

00:38:35.990 --> 00:38:39.320
than the current
alternative products.

00:38:39.320 --> 00:38:42.680
If these are real problems the
customer is trying to solve,

00:38:42.680 --> 00:38:45.110
he or she would have
already been looking

00:38:45.110 --> 00:38:46.400
for alternative solutions.

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:48.650
In fact, they may have already
been testing and paying

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:50.720
for solutions that
they're not happy with

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:53.030
and maybe even adopting them
and using because there's

00:38:53.030 --> 00:38:54.440
nothing else available.

00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:58.190
Your best customer,
the best person

00:38:58.190 --> 00:39:01.190
to understand what
they need, is someone

00:39:01.190 --> 00:39:03.890
that's already looking
for solutions, better yet,

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:05.870
has already paid for
alternative solutions

00:39:05.870 --> 00:39:07.580
that they're not happy with.

00:39:07.580 --> 00:39:10.065
Now you're not solving
the original problem,

00:39:10.065 --> 00:39:11.690
you're solving the
problem that they're

00:39:11.690 --> 00:39:16.250
having with the alternative
solutions that don't work well.

00:39:16.250 --> 00:39:19.790
All of a sudden now you've
shifted the priority

00:39:19.790 --> 00:39:22.790
and a higher level of
interest in your product

00:39:22.790 --> 00:39:25.650
if it can resolve
those types of issues.

00:39:25.650 --> 00:39:27.963
So ultimately, we're bringing
all of that together.

00:39:27.963 --> 00:39:29.630
So when we've captured
that information,

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:31.250
we can put it on a
graphic, something

00:39:31.250 --> 00:39:35.960
like this that says, well, how
are the pain relievers or pain

00:39:35.960 --> 00:39:38.150
elevators associated
with our products

00:39:38.150 --> 00:39:41.720
and services actually relieve
the pains of our customers

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:44.060
in order to allow them
to do their job better.

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:47.810
Or better yet, how do the
benefits of our solutions

00:39:47.810 --> 00:39:50.240
actually drive new
and improved scenarios

00:39:50.240 --> 00:39:55.590
that the customer can either
do more or do more with less.

00:39:55.590 --> 00:39:59.240
And so it requires two things, a
clear understanding of the jobs

00:39:59.240 --> 00:40:01.580
and the pains and
gains of the customer

00:40:01.580 --> 00:40:05.120
and then an evaluation of
how your product actually

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:07.160
matches or solves or fits--

00:40:07.160 --> 00:40:11.910
the word fit-- in order to
solve those pains and gains.

00:40:11.910 --> 00:40:16.160
And that's what we mean by the
notion of problem-solution fit.

00:40:16.160 --> 00:40:19.910
And this is why
Keurig didn't work,

00:40:19.910 --> 00:40:23.000
or the Keurig Kold anyway,
why it didn't work.

00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:25.520
I couldn't pinpoint exactly.

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:27.740
But I could tell you
just my own experience,

00:40:27.740 --> 00:40:30.920
I used to curate
for years, and I

00:40:30.920 --> 00:40:33.680
could have told you
immediately that the Kold made

00:40:33.680 --> 00:40:37.860
no sense to me, and I would
not be interested in it.

00:40:37.860 --> 00:40:40.820
So as we wrap up
here, after many weeks

00:40:40.820 --> 00:40:43.243
of this particular
program, you're

00:40:43.243 --> 00:40:45.410
going to be in for weeks
and weeks in these training

00:40:45.410 --> 00:40:47.450
workshops and the
research on your efforts

00:40:47.450 --> 00:40:49.700
and all the mentoring that
you're going to be getting.

00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:51.710
I found it interesting
if nothing's

00:40:51.710 --> 00:40:55.160
changed in the priorities or the
parameters of the competition,

00:40:55.160 --> 00:40:59.540
you're going to get five
minutes to tell your story.

00:40:59.540 --> 00:41:02.510
Multiple weeks of training
research and mentoring,

00:41:02.510 --> 00:41:06.870
and you're going to get five
minutes to tell your story.

00:41:06.870 --> 00:41:10.520
Now, the last thing
you want to do

00:41:10.520 --> 00:41:13.190
is try to fit all your
information into that five

00:41:13.190 --> 00:41:14.340
minutes.

00:41:14.340 --> 00:41:19.940
So I ask you, what
is the storyline?

00:41:19.940 --> 00:41:22.940
What is the compelling
and convincing storyline

00:41:22.940 --> 00:41:27.980
that you have to tell in order
to achieve a victory in a five

00:41:27.980 --> 00:41:30.770
minute presentation?

00:41:30.770 --> 00:41:32.660
Now, you might be
thinking I'm going to say

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:33.980
it's the value proposition.

00:41:33.980 --> 00:41:37.590
And I would tell
you it's not enough.

00:41:37.590 --> 00:41:41.810
It is the value proposition
in terms of its key role,

00:41:41.810 --> 00:41:45.530
but stay with me
for one more second.

00:41:45.530 --> 00:41:48.650
We have to understand
the customer.

00:41:48.650 --> 00:41:52.250
You only needed an idea to get
into the competition program.

00:41:52.250 --> 00:41:55.220
But to win, you're going
to have to leave your idea,

00:41:55.220 --> 00:41:57.740
leave it on the
desktop, and get out

00:41:57.740 --> 00:41:59.870
of the building, as
commonly referred to,

00:41:59.870 --> 00:42:04.440
virtually if necessary, and
talk directly to your customers.

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:08.910
Your business model, who is
your customer-- first of all,

00:42:08.910 --> 00:42:11.340
while you're evolving
business model,

00:42:11.340 --> 00:42:14.880
it is by its nature, a
value creation engine.

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:18.820
Therefore, everyone your
business touches, customers,

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:23.700
suppliers, end users,
payers, purchasers, whatever,

00:42:23.700 --> 00:42:26.160
however it's delineated
across from start

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:28.740
to finish on the decision
to buy your product instead

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:32.310
of one of your competitors,
those are your customers.

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:33.900
And if you need a
partner in order

00:42:33.900 --> 00:42:38.280
to deliver a unique feature
inside of a software,

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:40.440
they are part of your customer.

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:42.780
In other words, anyone
who derives value

00:42:42.780 --> 00:42:46.110
from participating with you
and your business proposition

00:42:46.110 --> 00:42:47.250
is a customer.

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:49.260
Remember, you're
doing this in order

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:51.990
to create and capture value.

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:54.390
There's many reasons
why some to sustain

00:42:54.390 --> 00:42:57.900
your enterprise but
others to change the world

00:42:57.900 --> 00:43:01.650
and make a meaningful impact
and sustain that change so it

00:43:01.650 --> 00:43:06.210
can impact people for years
to come, whatever it might be.

00:43:06.210 --> 00:43:09.270
As a value creation engine,
think of your enterprises

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:10.330
in that way.

00:43:10.330 --> 00:43:13.110
Anybody who drives
or contributes

00:43:13.110 --> 00:43:16.780
becomes a customer in your
customer discovery process.

00:43:16.780 --> 00:43:18.270
And here are the keys.

00:43:18.270 --> 00:43:21.090
You've got to acknowledge
the critical assumptions that

00:43:21.090 --> 00:43:23.190
undergird why you
think this is going

00:43:23.190 --> 00:43:24.660
to be a successful business.

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:29.080
And that means come clean, get
in a room with your team, Zoom

00:43:29.080 --> 00:43:31.170
room if it has to
be, get in a room

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:35.500
and come clean on everything
you're assuming to be true.

00:43:35.500 --> 00:43:38.130
In other words, ask yourself
what has to be true for this

00:43:38.130 --> 00:43:40.040
to be real?

00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:42.740
And then you're going to
document those, and then

00:43:42.740 --> 00:43:45.020
go out and empirically
test those directly

00:43:45.020 --> 00:43:46.850
with your potential customers.

00:43:46.850 --> 00:43:49.460
You need to be able to
articulate with evidence

00:43:49.460 --> 00:43:51.860
what problems they
are trying to solve,

00:43:51.860 --> 00:43:53.480
not what problem
you think they have

00:43:53.480 --> 00:43:55.790
but what problems are
they trying to solve.

00:43:55.790 --> 00:43:58.310
What alternative
solutions have they tried?

00:43:58.310 --> 00:43:59.930
And what value does
your technology

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:02.690
create if it solves
the problem better?

00:44:02.690 --> 00:44:04.970
Your focus must be
on understanding

00:44:04.970 --> 00:44:08.750
their past and current
behaviors, not opinions.

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:12.320
It doesn't matter if I said if
I had a product of this sort,

00:44:12.320 --> 00:44:13.130
would you buy one?

00:44:13.130 --> 00:44:14.630
And they said, oh, sure.

00:44:14.630 --> 00:44:16.430
It's a useless data.

00:44:16.430 --> 00:44:18.050
It's not even meaningful.

00:44:18.050 --> 00:44:19.760
Ask them what was
the last product they

00:44:19.760 --> 00:44:21.420
bought to try to solve this.

00:44:21.420 --> 00:44:22.550
How did that work out?

00:44:22.550 --> 00:44:24.080
Were they happy
with the decision?

00:44:24.080 --> 00:44:25.160
What didn't work out?

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:27.080
What are they looking for now?

00:44:27.080 --> 00:44:31.432
And what would they pay for
a solution that did that is--

00:44:31.432 --> 00:44:33.890
or what did they pay for the
solution that didn't work out?

00:44:33.890 --> 00:44:37.460
That is the notion of what they
perceive to be valuable or not.

00:44:37.460 --> 00:44:39.560
That's data you can trust.

00:44:39.560 --> 00:44:43.280
Their past behavior is the
key to proving the value

00:44:43.280 --> 00:44:46.100
in your proposed
product or service.

00:44:46.100 --> 00:44:48.050
And then, again, wrapping
up, as we've said,

00:44:48.050 --> 00:44:49.880
the value proposition,
your ability

00:44:49.880 --> 00:44:54.110
to prove that value proposition,
is the essential element

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:56.430
of how your whole business
model holds together.

00:44:56.430 --> 00:45:00.410
And remember, five minutes,
just five minutes you've got

00:45:00.410 --> 00:45:01.830
to get this across.

00:45:01.830 --> 00:45:04.070
In other words, you've got
to contract your space.

00:45:04.070 --> 00:45:05.720
You've got to bring
the message in.

00:45:05.720 --> 00:45:06.980
You've got to be very simple.

00:45:06.980 --> 00:45:10.010
But in that simple message,
it's got to be clear

00:45:10.010 --> 00:45:13.620
and it's got to be compelling
in order for you to win.

00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:17.570
OK, spoiler alert, I know
who the winner is this year.

00:45:17.570 --> 00:45:20.150
I'm sorry, Shannon
and Theo, I hate

00:45:20.150 --> 00:45:22.240
to let the cat out of the bag.

00:45:22.240 --> 00:45:24.950
But the winner this
year is the team

00:45:24.950 --> 00:45:27.410
that can prove the problem's
real, the market's large,

00:45:27.410 --> 00:45:29.120
the team can deliver,
and the solution

00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:32.550
ensures a strong and competitive
fit with customer needs.

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:35.440
In other words, can prove.

00:45:35.440 --> 00:45:36.970
You'll hear a lot
of presentations

00:45:36.970 --> 00:45:39.940
at the end of this program where
the team's still speculating,

00:45:39.940 --> 00:45:43.000
they think, they believe,
they're confident.

00:45:43.000 --> 00:45:45.730
The team that can prove
with evidence directly

00:45:45.730 --> 00:45:49.180
from customers that their
value proposition is real

00:45:49.180 --> 00:45:51.460
is going to heads and
shoulders above the others.

00:45:51.460 --> 00:45:53.600
And it's very clear in a
five minute presentation

00:45:53.600 --> 00:45:57.420
which ones are able
to achieve that.

00:45:57.420 --> 00:45:58.260
Well, I'm open.

00:45:58.260 --> 00:45:59.130
I think that was it.

00:45:59.130 --> 00:45:59.550
Yep.

00:45:59.550 --> 00:45:59.995
All right.

00:45:59.995 --> 00:46:00.828
Let the games begin.

00:46:00.828 --> 00:46:02.040
Let the games begin.

00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:02.968
Oh, fabulous.

00:46:02.968 --> 00:46:05.010
Do you mind if I pop this
back into gallery view,

00:46:05.010 --> 00:46:09.180
so we're sort of able
to converse a bit?

00:46:09.180 --> 00:46:10.350
Absolutely.

00:46:10.350 --> 00:46:11.055
All right.

00:46:11.055 --> 00:46:14.520
I hope there's a lot
of open conversation.

00:46:14.520 --> 00:46:15.540
All right.

00:46:15.540 --> 00:46:18.680
So does anybody have any
initial questions, thoughts?

00:46:18.680 --> 00:46:21.030
It would also be great to
see some people on camera.

00:46:21.030 --> 00:46:23.982
I am familiar with some
of you but not all of you.

00:46:23.982 --> 00:46:26.190
So it would be nice to sort
of see who is in the room

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:27.450
and see what kind
of conversation

00:46:27.450 --> 00:46:29.158
we can get going in
the next few minutes.

00:46:35.440 --> 00:46:37.020
Don't be shy.

00:46:37.020 --> 00:46:40.150
People are thinking, Shannon,
I have to ask, I hate to admit,

00:46:40.150 --> 00:46:41.730
I don't know what
that is behind you.

00:46:41.730 --> 00:46:42.605
What is your graphic?

00:46:42.605 --> 00:46:47.280
Ooh, OK, this is King's
College Chapel in Cambridge

00:46:47.280 --> 00:46:50.640
over in the UK, probably
my favorite place

00:46:50.640 --> 00:46:51.810
that I've ever sung.

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:54.000
Because the acoustics
on those fan

00:46:54.000 --> 00:46:56.490
vault ceilings are unparalleled.

00:46:56.490 --> 00:46:59.490
I've never sung anywhere
in the states, maybe

00:46:59.490 --> 00:47:01.380
the cathedral of
St. John the Divine,

00:47:01.380 --> 00:47:03.120
because it's just so huge.

00:47:03.120 --> 00:47:07.230
But any of the places where
I've done nice singing,

00:47:07.230 --> 00:47:08.940
this takes the cake.

00:47:08.940 --> 00:47:11.980
That is awesome.

00:47:11.980 --> 00:47:13.030
All right.

00:47:13.030 --> 00:47:15.250
Well, for the
benefit of people who

00:47:15.250 --> 00:47:16.720
are watching this
asynchronously,

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:21.520
I think one question in order
to avoid it being a Keurig

00:47:21.520 --> 00:47:23.920
and kind of continuing
to kind of bark up a tree

00:47:23.920 --> 00:47:28.060
that maybe doesn't have
value, how should teams

00:47:28.060 --> 00:47:32.260
think about potentially changing
their big why or their value

00:47:32.260 --> 00:47:32.800
problem?

00:47:32.800 --> 00:47:34.690
What's the best
strategies for doing that,

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:37.750
and how can you do that
effectively while still

00:47:37.750 --> 00:47:42.160
kind of preserving
your initial mission?

00:47:42.160 --> 00:47:45.910
Yeah, that's a
really good question.

00:47:45.910 --> 00:47:47.950
Just to be as simple
about it, I didn't

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:53.110
discuss the notion of a term
we refer to lean startup.

00:47:53.110 --> 00:47:55.870
Again, it might be a common
term your teams or students have

00:47:55.870 --> 00:47:56.830
used.

00:47:56.830 --> 00:48:01.090
But the notion of this
customer discovery activity

00:48:01.090 --> 00:48:04.310
is because we're
trying to go fast.

00:48:04.310 --> 00:48:06.640
In other words, we want
to get to the nose.

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:08.890
We know there's a lot
of the assumptions we're

00:48:08.890 --> 00:48:11.860
making that aren't
real or aren't true.

00:48:11.860 --> 00:48:14.500
And in the process of doing
it effectively and doing it

00:48:14.500 --> 00:48:16.250
deliberately, we're
going to uncover.

00:48:16.250 --> 00:48:17.800
Where those gaps are.

00:48:17.800 --> 00:48:21.070
So trust me, all of your
ideas are going to pivot.

00:48:21.070 --> 00:48:25.210
They're all going to modify
either slightly or greatly.

00:48:25.210 --> 00:48:28.720
But the how we do
that is actually

00:48:28.720 --> 00:48:31.252
derived from the
discovery process itself.

00:48:31.252 --> 00:48:32.710
I don't want you
to think that this

00:48:32.710 --> 00:48:34.668
is I'm going to go talk
to a bunch of customers

00:48:34.668 --> 00:48:36.910
and realize my business
is dead in the water.

00:48:36.910 --> 00:48:38.020
Nobody needs what I have.

00:48:38.020 --> 00:48:39.670
What I'm going to do now?

00:48:39.670 --> 00:48:41.830
It's actually in the
discovery process

00:48:41.830 --> 00:48:43.780
by really being
honest with yourself

00:48:43.780 --> 00:48:46.330
not trying to simply validate
that you have the greatest

00:48:46.330 --> 00:48:49.750
solution in the world but really
understanding their problems

00:48:49.750 --> 00:48:52.450
and understanding how they look
for alternative solutions now

00:48:52.450 --> 00:48:54.575
and what they're willing
to pay for those solutions

00:48:54.575 --> 00:48:57.070
where the next idea will emerge.

00:48:57.070 --> 00:49:00.610
I tell my teams all the time,
the last thing you want to do

00:49:00.610 --> 00:49:05.613
is go in with I've got
a one-step solution,

00:49:05.613 --> 00:49:07.030
if you don't want
it, you must not

00:49:07.030 --> 00:49:12.010
be smart enough to know you
need it, or I'm out of business,

00:49:12.010 --> 00:49:14.020
that neither of those are true.

00:49:14.020 --> 00:49:16.188
You're going to learn
through the discovery process

00:49:16.188 --> 00:49:17.980
whether your initial
assumptions are right.

00:49:17.980 --> 00:49:20.860
But that data is also
going to tell you where

00:49:20.860 --> 00:49:22.600
your appropriate pivots are.

00:49:22.600 --> 00:49:26.440
I've had teams go all the way
to completely different market

00:49:26.440 --> 00:49:27.340
segments.

00:49:27.340 --> 00:49:29.410
I could share the
stories if we had time.

00:49:29.410 --> 00:49:33.430
But literally from one
medical segment to a drone.

00:49:33.430 --> 00:49:36.520
They were now doing
battery-powered systems

00:49:36.520 --> 00:49:39.490
for drones all the way
from heart implants,

00:49:39.490 --> 00:49:41.140
literally in a five week period.

00:49:41.140 --> 00:49:42.190
And how do they do that?

00:49:42.190 --> 00:49:44.110
Were they jumping
around making--

00:49:44.110 --> 00:49:46.240
no, they started with
a core assumption

00:49:46.240 --> 00:49:48.640
they believed in, tested
it, invalidated it,

00:49:48.640 --> 00:49:51.415
and what they learned from that
validation or invalidation told

00:49:51.415 --> 00:49:55.010
them where to go next.

00:49:55.010 --> 00:49:57.100
So I hope that's a part answer.

00:49:57.100 --> 00:49:59.490
That's a great example.

00:49:59.490 --> 00:50:00.790
That's a great example.

00:50:00.790 --> 00:50:02.858
And I think that's
helpful now as people

00:50:02.858 --> 00:50:04.150
are coming up with their ideas.

00:50:04.150 --> 00:50:05.860
Because we're going
to be catering

00:50:05.860 --> 00:50:08.650
to people in a for profit
track and a nonprofit track.

00:50:08.650 --> 00:50:11.260
And I know that mission
statements are the underpinning

00:50:11.260 --> 00:50:16.370
things of a nonprofit and
mission wonder or mission stray

00:50:16.370 --> 00:50:17.840
can be a serious problem.

00:50:17.840 --> 00:50:21.910
So the importance of
testing your idea up front

00:50:21.910 --> 00:50:25.000
to make sure that you're
staying faithful to that,

00:50:25.000 --> 00:50:27.990
there's a lot of back end work
that's going to need to happen.

00:50:27.990 --> 00:50:30.640
I think that nonprofits don't
have the same level of agility

00:50:30.640 --> 00:50:33.310
to be able to
switch necessarily.

00:50:33.310 --> 00:50:35.530
So I think that's
an important thing

00:50:35.530 --> 00:50:38.545
for any of my nonprofit
people out there to remember.

00:50:38.545 --> 00:50:39.390
Yeah, absolutely.

00:50:39.390 --> 00:50:40.640
Thank you for mentioning that.

00:50:40.640 --> 00:50:42.500
I wasn't aware you
would have nonprofits.

00:50:42.500 --> 00:50:44.570
And that's a great point.

00:50:44.570 --> 00:50:46.960
Let's see, I have--

00:50:46.960 --> 00:50:51.400
we work a lot through a
very iterative process.

00:50:51.400 --> 00:50:53.170
There is a lot of mentoring.

00:50:53.170 --> 00:50:56.440
We do a lot of pitch workshops
so that people start--

00:50:56.440 --> 00:50:58.630
they start writing,
they start riffing,

00:50:58.630 --> 00:51:01.930
and then they start getting more
cohesive their pitch scripts.

00:51:01.930 --> 00:51:04.930
And the before and
afters tend to be really

00:51:04.930 --> 00:51:06.330
powerful with the students.

00:51:06.330 --> 00:51:09.910
So I'm wondering if you've got
an example of a venture that's

00:51:09.910 --> 00:51:13.240
sort of like the what not to
wear that ended up turning

00:51:13.240 --> 00:51:14.770
around and being successful.

00:51:14.770 --> 00:51:17.560
Do you have like a
good before and after

00:51:17.560 --> 00:51:20.410
of a team that might not
have had a clear value prop

00:51:20.410 --> 00:51:24.080
in the beginning but was
able to pivot and succeed?

00:51:24.080 --> 00:51:26.650
I have lots of examples.

00:51:26.650 --> 00:51:29.210
I was thinking-- as
you're saying that,

00:51:29.210 --> 00:51:30.370
I was thinking about--

00:51:30.370 --> 00:51:34.090
the ones that come to
mind, for some reason,

00:51:34.090 --> 00:51:37.630
are mostly medical.

00:51:37.630 --> 00:51:40.570
Mostly because we've just
finished up our I-Corps cohort.

00:51:40.570 --> 00:51:43.690
And this was a particular team.

00:51:43.690 --> 00:51:46.600
They went in, they
had an adhesive,

00:51:46.600 --> 00:51:52.300
a medical bandage adhesive that
their problem they were trying

00:51:52.300 --> 00:51:56.410
to address was when you have
an open wound or a burn victim,

00:51:56.410 --> 00:52:01.240
the actually redressing of
those wounds tears the part

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:05.423
of the healed skin off when
they're-- so it's very painful.

00:52:05.423 --> 00:52:07.090
And they were trying
to address the pain

00:52:07.090 --> 00:52:09.940
by developing an adhesive
onto this bandage that

00:52:09.940 --> 00:52:13.960
would break down under light,
a certain type of UV light.

00:52:13.960 --> 00:52:17.170
And they went to nurses
that were treating

00:52:17.170 --> 00:52:20.410
patients on wound care
floors and the nurses said,

00:52:20.410 --> 00:52:21.850
we don't care.

00:52:21.850 --> 00:52:23.770
They said, aren't you
worried about the pain?

00:52:23.770 --> 00:52:25.540
No, we don't care
about the pain.

00:52:25.540 --> 00:52:27.512
This is 35 customer
interviews in.

00:52:27.512 --> 00:52:28.720
They said, no, we don't care.

00:52:28.720 --> 00:52:29.470
And it's like, what do you mean?

00:52:29.470 --> 00:52:31.400
Well, of course we
care, generally.

00:52:31.400 --> 00:52:33.700
But we have to pick one
of those two bandages

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:35.380
that are in the
cabinet over there.

00:52:35.380 --> 00:52:37.690
We don't have any power
or influence ability.

00:52:37.690 --> 00:52:41.200
So they completely had to
pivot out of that segment.

00:52:41.200 --> 00:52:43.630
And moving from the
wound care nurses,

00:52:43.630 --> 00:52:46.448
they went directly to the
head wound care nurse.

00:52:46.448 --> 00:52:48.490
They said, well, how do
we get different bandages

00:52:48.490 --> 00:52:49.360
into that cabinet?

00:52:49.360 --> 00:52:50.897
And she said, I don't care.

00:52:50.897 --> 00:52:53.230
And they're like, well, what
do you mean you don't care?

00:52:53.230 --> 00:52:54.880
Well, of course I
care that there's pain

00:52:54.880 --> 00:52:56.320
and that there should be
alternative solutions.

00:52:56.320 --> 00:52:58.270
But we have to go
with the ones that

00:52:58.270 --> 00:53:01.420
are approved cost-wise
that fit into that cabinet.

00:53:01.420 --> 00:53:05.560
Now they are 65 interviews in,
and there have been demoralized

00:53:05.560 --> 00:53:07.460
one step along the way.

00:53:07.460 --> 00:53:10.570
And at the end, they
eventually they said, well,

00:53:10.570 --> 00:53:12.940
who decides which ones
go into those cabinets?

00:53:12.940 --> 00:53:17.825
And it was the reimbursement
officer upstairs.

00:53:17.825 --> 00:53:20.200
And they ended up with the
reimbursement officers going--

00:53:20.200 --> 00:53:23.740
and he's desperate for
anything, any solution that

00:53:23.740 --> 00:53:25.090
could resolve pain.

00:53:25.090 --> 00:53:27.190
He's willing to put a
more expensive bandage

00:53:27.190 --> 00:53:30.100
into that cabinet if it's going
to reduce the pain scores,

00:53:30.100 --> 00:53:33.010
because he's reimbursed,
the hospital system

00:53:33.010 --> 00:53:34.580
is reimbursed off
of the pain scores.

00:53:34.580 --> 00:53:35.705
See, they didn't know that.

00:53:35.705 --> 00:53:36.940
They didn't understand that.

00:53:36.940 --> 00:53:40.810
They thought the total solution
was just immediately addressing

00:53:40.810 --> 00:53:42.580
the pain that the patient had.

00:53:42.580 --> 00:53:45.130
But only through up
to 80 interviews now

00:53:45.130 --> 00:53:47.410
before they realized they
have a bigger solution

00:53:47.410 --> 00:53:49.180
than they ever could imagine.

00:53:49.180 --> 00:53:51.580
And they actually had to
go back into the laboratory

00:53:51.580 --> 00:53:53.410
and readdress the
adhesive, because he

00:53:53.410 --> 00:53:56.398
wanted it to be a stronger
adhesive but breakdown quicker.

00:53:56.398 --> 00:53:57.940
And they were already
re-engineering.

00:53:57.940 --> 00:54:00.593
So that team went on
to start a company.

00:54:00.593 --> 00:54:03.010
They raised several million
dollars, got their new product

00:54:03.010 --> 00:54:03.510
issue.

00:54:03.510 --> 00:54:05.650
They're an FDA
regulatory pathway now.

00:54:05.650 --> 00:54:08.380
And they would have never known
if they had just simply built

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:11.650
the product and tried to sell
it to wound care clinics,

00:54:11.650 --> 00:54:13.550
they would have never
figured that out.

00:54:13.550 --> 00:54:16.642
I hope that's a little
bit of an example.

00:54:16.642 --> 00:54:18.100
I'm just sort of
trying to get over

00:54:18.100 --> 00:54:20.680
my personal squeamish
factor to see

00:54:20.680 --> 00:54:23.510
the heart of the story,
which was fantastic.

00:54:23.510 --> 00:54:24.920
Sorry about that.

00:54:24.920 --> 00:54:26.080
No, not at all.

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:27.940
Yeah, there's several others.

00:54:27.940 --> 00:54:31.870
The one I mentioned about there
was a heart, not a transplant

00:54:31.870 --> 00:54:32.890
but a heart pacemaker.

00:54:32.890 --> 00:54:36.550
They had come up with a
way of a permanent battery

00:54:36.550 --> 00:54:39.070
getting power off the flow
of blood through the heart

00:54:39.070 --> 00:54:42.190
and transitioning that
power back to the pacemaker.

00:54:42.190 --> 00:54:44.470
And the problem is that's
an interesting idea,

00:54:44.470 --> 00:54:46.060
because you hate
to get a pacemaker

00:54:46.060 --> 00:54:47.650
and 20 years later
have to open up,

00:54:47.650 --> 00:54:50.890
do another open-heart
surgery to get it repaired

00:54:50.890 --> 00:54:52.600
or get the battery replaced.

00:54:52.600 --> 00:54:54.890
But they found out the
people don't get pacemakers

00:54:54.890 --> 00:54:55.910
until their late '70s.

00:54:55.910 --> 00:54:59.670
And they don't live long enough
for the batteries to go down.

00:54:59.670 --> 00:55:01.590
So there was no need for that.

00:55:01.590 --> 00:55:02.120
However--

00:55:02.120 --> 00:55:04.880
That is a solution
looking for a problem.

00:55:04.880 --> 00:55:08.100
--the same technology became
a turbine to drive drones.

00:55:08.100 --> 00:55:11.570
And so you could repower
the batteries in a drone.

00:55:11.570 --> 00:55:12.650
They assess themselves.

00:55:12.650 --> 00:55:15.320
Well, what other technologies
have battery issues?

00:55:15.320 --> 00:55:16.790
And there were a number of them.

00:55:16.790 --> 00:55:18.332
And that was the
one they focused on,

00:55:18.332 --> 00:55:20.798
so a completely
different [INAUDIBLE]..

00:55:20.798 --> 00:55:22.140
Wow.

00:55:22.140 --> 00:55:24.750
So just a couple examples.

00:55:24.750 --> 00:55:30.620
So you spoke a little
while ago about basically--

00:55:30.620 --> 00:55:33.470
oh, you know what, I know
we're running out of time.

00:55:33.470 --> 00:55:35.440
I had one final question.

00:55:35.440 --> 00:55:37.105
No, I have two
questions actually.

00:55:37.105 --> 00:55:38.230
Is that all right with you?

00:55:38.230 --> 00:55:39.300
No, it's fine by me.

00:55:39.300 --> 00:55:40.230
Sure, sure.

00:55:40.230 --> 00:55:44.210
OK, firstly, we're talking
about how other partners--

00:55:44.210 --> 00:55:45.620
it's not just direct partners.

00:55:45.620 --> 00:55:47.780
It's just not consumers.

00:55:47.780 --> 00:55:49.460
There are a lot
of people that are

00:55:49.460 --> 00:55:50.900
integrated into the success.

00:55:50.900 --> 00:55:52.460
You were saying it
took 80 interviews

00:55:52.460 --> 00:55:55.471
to get to the guy upstairs
who really had the answer.

00:55:55.471 --> 00:56:01.140
So do you know as far as those
partnerships are concerned,

00:56:01.140 --> 00:56:02.270
that's really important.

00:56:02.270 --> 00:56:04.850
But also, when you're
creating your own team,

00:56:04.850 --> 00:56:07.790
partnerships are
incredibly important.

00:56:07.790 --> 00:56:10.130
How does one go
about identifying

00:56:10.130 --> 00:56:13.280
the right team and the right
partners that can contribute to

00:56:13.280 --> 00:56:16.340
or are aligned with
the value problem?

00:56:16.340 --> 00:56:20.760
Yeah, that's a
really good question.

00:56:20.760 --> 00:56:23.090
You know, there is a
bit of a conundrum,

00:56:23.090 --> 00:56:26.570
because a lot of
times the value prop

00:56:26.570 --> 00:56:29.330
is delivered by the
underlying technology.

00:56:29.330 --> 00:56:31.260
But the underlying
technology is designed,

00:56:31.260 --> 00:56:34.670
developed, maintained, and
implemented by the team.

00:56:34.670 --> 00:56:37.280
And so to a large
extent, the team

00:56:37.280 --> 00:56:39.960
is fundamental to the
value proposition.

00:56:39.960 --> 00:56:44.230
So if what you have now is
an idea but no solution,

00:56:44.230 --> 00:56:46.100
you have an idea for
how you would solve

00:56:46.100 --> 00:56:48.800
a particular problem, but
you don't have the solution,

00:56:48.800 --> 00:56:51.680
you're in a pretty
good place in order

00:56:51.680 --> 00:56:56.120
to identify through the
customer discovery activity

00:56:56.120 --> 00:56:58.880
what is the unique
and compelling problem

00:56:58.880 --> 00:57:01.378
and how does that manifest
in terms of their pain.

00:57:01.378 --> 00:57:03.170
What does it limit the
customer from doing?

00:57:03.170 --> 00:57:04.880
And what does, if
it was resolved,

00:57:04.880 --> 00:57:08.580
what else could they be
doing more effectively?

00:57:08.580 --> 00:57:13.310
And then that speaks very
vividly to the clear features

00:57:13.310 --> 00:57:15.920
and attributes and
deliverables that you have

00:57:15.920 --> 00:57:18.420
to have in that final product.

00:57:18.420 --> 00:57:22.920
So if you're one member of what
needs to be a three member team

00:57:22.920 --> 00:57:24.920
and you're doing this
assessment and you come up

00:57:24.920 --> 00:57:26.600
with a clear
articulation of what

00:57:26.600 --> 00:57:28.850
those compelling
attributes are, you

00:57:28.850 --> 00:57:32.845
can then do a similar type of
activity in backward fashion

00:57:32.845 --> 00:57:35.270
as to identify where are
the key strengths, what do

00:57:35.270 --> 00:57:37.458
I need with respect to these--

00:57:37.458 --> 00:57:39.500
I mentioned in the business,
those key activities

00:57:39.500 --> 00:57:40.490
and key resources.

00:57:40.490 --> 00:57:43.070
Sometimes you need a
key database in order

00:57:43.070 --> 00:57:45.590
to drive a pricing model.

00:57:45.590 --> 00:57:47.820
Or you need someone to
build something like that.

00:57:47.820 --> 00:57:49.622
And if you can
articulate that based

00:57:49.622 --> 00:57:51.830
on what you heard directly
from the customer in terms

00:57:51.830 --> 00:57:54.590
of the clear, compelling
features and attributes

00:57:54.590 --> 00:57:57.530
they would need in order
to deliver that key value,

00:57:57.530 --> 00:57:59.300
you can articulate
what the skill

00:57:59.300 --> 00:58:03.590
sets are that are going to be
necessary in the ultimate team.

00:58:03.590 --> 00:58:07.250
If you're not a team
already going in,

00:58:07.250 --> 00:58:09.290
or better yet, even
if you are, if you're

00:58:09.290 --> 00:58:12.860
open to part of the learning,
part of the discovery being

00:58:12.860 --> 00:58:15.590
that the team has a
gaping weakness in terms

00:58:15.590 --> 00:58:18.113
of its ability to
deliver the value,

00:58:18.113 --> 00:58:19.655
you ought to be
honest with yourself,

00:58:19.655 --> 00:58:22.590
and say we've got to cap
that, we've got to fix it.

00:58:22.590 --> 00:58:24.452
And again, if
you're clear enough

00:58:24.452 --> 00:58:25.910
in what the customer
needs, I think

00:58:25.910 --> 00:58:28.118
you'll be clear enough in
understanding and isolating

00:58:28.118 --> 00:58:29.270
those skill sets.

00:58:29.270 --> 00:58:32.930
And from there, it just becomes
a networking and marketing

00:58:32.930 --> 00:58:35.240
and recruiting type
of activity that I

00:58:35.240 --> 00:58:39.510
think is we're all
pretty familiar with.

00:58:39.510 --> 00:58:40.010
Excellent.

00:58:40.010 --> 00:58:42.710
It's not an easy thing though.

00:58:42.710 --> 00:58:45.830
I'm usually dealing with
teams who are fixed going in.

00:58:45.830 --> 00:58:47.810
And when they realize
there's a gap,

00:58:47.810 --> 00:58:49.670
they look for an
external partner.

00:58:49.670 --> 00:58:52.610
And they're never open, as
maybe many of your teams

00:58:52.610 --> 00:58:56.450
will be, open to reshaping
the fundamental team itself.

00:58:56.450 --> 00:58:58.350
That's a great concept.

00:58:58.350 --> 00:59:00.080
I think that's an
important takeaway too.

00:59:00.080 --> 00:59:02.720
We've got a lot of students
who might have a great idea who

00:59:02.720 --> 00:59:05.060
don't know who their
teammates need to be,

00:59:05.060 --> 00:59:08.110
or we might have groups that are
looking for that other person.

00:59:08.110 --> 00:59:10.790
So we're trying to facilitate
the kind of networking that

00:59:10.790 --> 00:59:13.580
will enable people to
have awareness of this,

00:59:13.580 --> 00:59:15.620
have the self-awareness
to be able to seek

00:59:15.620 --> 00:59:18.230
the right teammates and
for people in the community

00:59:18.230 --> 00:59:19.910
to have awareness
of this opportunity,

00:59:19.910 --> 00:59:23.150
so that people can
find those natural fits

00:59:23.150 --> 00:59:24.785
and those critical partners.

00:59:24.785 --> 00:59:26.960
Yeah, and sometimes it
takes an intermediary

00:59:26.960 --> 00:59:29.690
like you to facilitate that.

00:59:29.690 --> 00:59:30.740
That's challenging.

00:59:30.740 --> 00:59:33.200
Even if I am open
to hearing that I

00:59:33.200 --> 00:59:36.860
need help finding that
specific person or persons,

00:59:36.860 --> 00:59:38.270
that can be challenging.

00:59:38.270 --> 00:59:40.920
So yeah, we don't have
that at Ohio State.

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:43.180
So I may call on you.

00:59:43.180 --> 00:59:44.992
Absolutely.

00:59:44.992 --> 00:59:46.880
We see a hundred ideas a year.

00:59:46.880 --> 00:59:52.220
Actually, it's worth mentioning
that the competition has

00:59:52.220 --> 00:59:53.450
primarily been in-person.

00:59:53.450 --> 00:59:56.660
We pivoted sort of
as gracefully as we

00:59:56.660 --> 00:59:59.090
could in the spring
for obvious reasons.

00:59:59.090 --> 01:00:03.090
We're doing the entire
program virtually this year.

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:05.120
Most of the MIT
students are off campus.

01:00:05.120 --> 01:00:07.020
It's a year-ish long program.

01:00:07.020 --> 01:00:10.020
So we want to make it fair
and equitable for everybody.

01:00:10.020 --> 01:00:15.530
So to that end, students
anywhere can be part of teams.

01:00:15.530 --> 01:00:17.910
Teams just have to
have one MIT student.

01:00:17.910 --> 01:00:21.140
So if you have any students who
might be interested in this,

01:00:21.140 --> 01:00:23.030
we certainly can try
to broker something

01:00:23.030 --> 01:00:24.740
to get this on the map.

01:00:24.740 --> 01:00:27.290
Because at least we're
Zoom is concerned,

01:00:27.290 --> 01:00:30.140
there's like the sky's
the limit on these teams

01:00:30.140 --> 01:00:31.170
and partnerships.

01:00:31.170 --> 01:00:36.350
And what's the deadline for when
the team has to be finalized?

01:00:36.350 --> 01:00:37.510
That's a great question.

01:00:37.510 --> 01:00:40.250
The applications are due
the first week of February.

01:00:40.250 --> 01:00:44.270
But I think that as the
projects and programs sort of go

01:00:44.270 --> 01:00:47.690
through various
iterations, I think

01:00:47.690 --> 01:00:50.240
we should have a great idea
of what teams are roughly

01:00:50.240 --> 01:00:51.650
by the first week of February.

01:00:51.650 --> 01:00:54.140
But there could
still be a little bit

01:00:54.140 --> 01:00:56.630
through one-on-one
mentorships, students

01:00:56.630 --> 01:01:00.180
might figure out that we really
need another person here.

01:01:00.180 --> 01:01:03.140
So in order to
maximize success, we

01:01:03.140 --> 01:01:08.500
are probably open to the idea
of adding people as needed.

01:01:08.500 --> 01:01:09.500
OK, great.

01:01:09.500 --> 01:01:11.792
No, I definitely would
have people interested.

01:01:11.792 --> 01:01:12.500
So if you could--

01:01:12.500 --> 01:01:13.970
Perfect.

01:01:13.970 --> 01:01:15.500
--stay connected, let me know.

01:01:15.500 --> 01:01:16.970
Let's do that.

01:01:16.970 --> 01:01:20.222
Theo, did you have one final
question before we wrap up?

01:01:20.222 --> 01:01:20.930
Yeah, absolutely.

01:01:20.930 --> 01:01:22.805
So Dr. Camp, I think
you started to reference

01:01:22.805 --> 01:01:25.490
this in your spoilers.

01:01:25.490 --> 01:01:27.230
But I think if you have any--

01:01:27.230 --> 01:01:29.930
if you could share one
kind of pearl of wisdom

01:01:29.930 --> 01:01:32.990
or one key takeaway for our
teams in this competition,

01:01:32.990 --> 01:01:35.660
I think that would be really
helpful for the teams here

01:01:35.660 --> 01:01:38.330
and those watching
asynchronously.

01:01:38.330 --> 01:01:39.500
Yeah, no, thanks.

01:01:39.500 --> 01:01:41.600
That's a great way to wrap up.

01:01:41.600 --> 01:01:43.850
You know, in
thinking about that,

01:01:43.850 --> 01:01:50.060
there's so many pieces
that go together.

01:01:50.060 --> 01:01:53.300
And I don't know if you
guys put more weight

01:01:53.300 --> 01:01:57.200
on the final presentation in
terms of determining winners

01:01:57.200 --> 01:02:00.690
or not, or if it's just
kind of a cumulative effect

01:02:00.690 --> 01:02:02.870
as teams move
through the program,

01:02:02.870 --> 01:02:08.210
but the ability to tell
an effective story--

01:02:08.210 --> 01:02:11.570
and when I, again, heard
that it's about a five minute

01:02:11.570 --> 01:02:14.180
presentation with the
five minutes of Q&amp;A,

01:02:14.180 --> 01:02:17.750
I want to emphasize
that if the team's not

01:02:17.750 --> 01:02:21.950
able to tell the story in a
compelling convincing fashion

01:02:21.950 --> 01:02:23.480
in the five minutes,
they'd better

01:02:23.480 --> 01:02:27.000
be able to answer the questions
in a compelling and convincing

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:27.500
way.

01:02:27.500 --> 01:02:29.990
Because they're going
to get the key questions

01:02:29.990 --> 01:02:32.690
that the reviewers, judges,
whatever they're called

01:02:32.690 --> 01:02:34.910
are going to be listening for.

01:02:34.910 --> 01:02:42.470
However, my advice would be by
getting familiar and testing

01:02:42.470 --> 01:02:44.630
very clearly and
honestly, openly

01:02:44.630 --> 01:02:47.420
questioning and challenging
your own understanding

01:02:47.420 --> 01:02:49.520
and underlying
assumptions, you're

01:02:49.520 --> 01:02:51.350
going to have a much
more compelling way

01:02:51.350 --> 01:02:56.000
to tell the key considerations,
whether it be market that

01:02:56.000 --> 01:02:59.240
differentiates your opportunity
or it's the compelling solution

01:02:59.240 --> 01:03:04.310
or whether it be just kind of
economic or social or human

01:03:04.310 --> 01:03:06.800
impact you're able
to have, you need

01:03:06.800 --> 01:03:09.140
to isolate and be
able to articulate

01:03:09.140 --> 01:03:14.360
what that really moving
moment that moving element is.

01:03:14.360 --> 01:03:18.560
It's not enough to just
dotting I's and crossing T's.

01:03:18.560 --> 01:03:22.010
There's probably so many good
ideas in this competition.

01:03:22.010 --> 01:03:24.320
The team that can
get that idea across

01:03:24.320 --> 01:03:27.800
in a believable realistic way--

01:03:27.800 --> 01:03:31.970
and the best believability is
that if you've either done it,

01:03:31.970 --> 01:03:36.920
or if you haven't done it,
you have connected directly

01:03:36.920 --> 01:03:38.840
with those end
users and partners,

01:03:38.840 --> 01:03:40.430
so that they'll
tell you-- those who

01:03:40.430 --> 01:03:43.330
have done it can tell
you that this is doable,

01:03:43.330 --> 01:03:44.600
this is believable.

01:03:44.600 --> 01:03:49.130
In other words, it's making
it real, as real as possible.

01:03:49.130 --> 01:03:52.760
And the moment you have to
say, I think, I believe,

01:03:52.760 --> 01:03:55.180
you realize that's
a compelling gap.

01:03:55.180 --> 01:03:58.430
That's an area of challenge
that you're going to have.

01:03:58.430 --> 01:04:00.410
Making it real,
making it concise,

01:04:00.410 --> 01:04:01.970
hitting the critical
elements that

01:04:01.970 --> 01:04:04.460
make your opportunity
differentiated

01:04:04.460 --> 01:04:07.490
from the others--

01:04:07.490 --> 01:04:09.270
and because now
you've done that,

01:04:09.270 --> 01:04:12.260
you'll have so much
more confidence in terms

01:04:12.260 --> 01:04:14.420
of how you tell that story.

01:04:14.420 --> 01:04:16.900
It doesn't sound like one thing.

01:04:16.900 --> 01:04:19.220
That was the best
possible answer.

01:04:19.220 --> 01:04:21.800
Now anybody who is here
for our kickoff conference

01:04:21.800 --> 01:04:23.900
and keynotes and
anybody who's watching

01:04:23.900 --> 01:04:27.860
who will watch this who saw
that first event, it all

01:04:27.860 --> 01:04:29.600
comes down to storytelling.

01:04:29.600 --> 01:04:33.110
There are so many
entrepreneurship innovation

01:04:33.110 --> 01:04:35.870
initiatives at the
Institute, and everybody has

01:04:35.870 --> 01:04:37.460
their own sort of value added.

01:04:37.460 --> 01:04:39.980
And apart from
being arts-driven,

01:04:39.980 --> 01:04:42.770
the thing that people come
here and the big takeaway

01:04:42.770 --> 01:04:45.050
is storytelling.

01:04:45.050 --> 01:04:48.500
It's sort of the underpinning
of the entire operation.

01:04:48.500 --> 01:04:53.000
So I promise we didn't
feed Dr. Camp that answer.

01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:56.300
It just it's so perfectly
within the theme

01:04:56.300 --> 01:05:00.320
of being successful with
helping to launch your venture.

01:05:00.320 --> 01:05:03.025
So that couldn't
have been better.

01:05:03.025 --> 01:05:04.725
[LAUGHS]

01:05:04.725 --> 01:05:06.290
And with the
cathedral background,

01:05:06.290 --> 01:05:08.360
I've got to say, Amen.

01:05:08.360 --> 01:05:10.320
Exactly.

01:05:10.320 --> 01:05:12.274
Hey, if I can be
at the final event,

01:05:12.274 --> 01:05:14.274
I'd love to see some of
those great stories that

01:05:14.274 --> 01:05:14.515
are going to get told.

01:05:14.515 --> 01:05:15.700
Oh, absolutely.

01:05:15.700 --> 01:05:17.240
We will definitely be in touch.

01:05:17.240 --> 01:05:17.907
Just as a guest.

01:05:17.907 --> 01:05:19.890
That would be fantastic.

01:05:19.890 --> 01:05:24.830
So if any of the three of you
stalwart soldiers here have

01:05:24.830 --> 01:05:27.180
any questions, let me know.

01:05:27.180 --> 01:05:29.420
We can also forward
things on to Dr. Camp.

01:05:29.420 --> 01:05:32.750
So if that is it for
everybody, then I'm

01:05:32.750 --> 01:05:35.780
just going to thank
Dr. Camp for his time.

01:05:35.780 --> 01:05:37.680
It was fantastic
having you here.

01:05:37.680 --> 01:05:39.390
We really appreciate this.

01:05:39.390 --> 01:05:42.710
And it was so engaging,
especially even

01:05:42.710 --> 01:05:43.850
for Zoom, you know?

01:05:43.850 --> 01:05:45.810
It was it was wonderful.

01:05:45.810 --> 01:05:47.210
We can't thank you enough.

01:05:47.210 --> 01:05:48.480
Well, glad to be with you.

01:05:48.480 --> 01:05:49.100
Thanks, again.

01:05:49.100 --> 01:05:49.670
Perfect.

01:05:49.670 --> 01:05:50.170
Thank you.

01:05:50.170 --> 01:05:51.500
Thank you so much.

